1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:06,320
After my last video on how cursor works and why SpaceX is looking at it, many of you asked for a proper deep dive.

2
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:17,680
So in this video, I'll show you how engineers actually use cursor end to end, including workflows, mental models, and 25 practical tips you can apply immediately. Let's get into it.

3
00:00:23,438 --> 00:00:24,720
Here is what we are covering today.

4
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:27,748
We start with what cursor actually is and how it thinks.

5
00:00:28,174 --> 00:00:30,340
So you have the right mental model from the start.

6
00:00:30,798 --> 00:00:34,820
Then we cover the three interaction models tab, command k, and chat.

7
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:40,388
Each one has a specific use case, and knowing when to reach for which one saves you a lot of time.

8
00:00:40,782 --> 00:00:48,132
Then we go deep on plan mode and agent mode, the two-phase workflow that separates professional usage from just winging it with AI.

9
00:00:48,558 --> 00:00:56,740
Then rules, commands, and memories, the most underused feature in cursor, and the ones that give you the biggest productivity gain, especially on a team.

10
00:00:57,070 --> 00:01:03,588
And finally, the advanced workflows, parallel agents, background agents, bug bot, and MCP integrations.

11
00:01:04,014 --> 00:01:07,812
Throughout each section, I will draw practical tips you can apply immediately.

12
00:01:08,270 --> 00:01:11,780
By the end of this, you will have a completely different relationship with your IDE.

13
00:01:12,078 --> 00:01:18,400
Now throughout the course, I will be working on a real project, a task management API built with Fast API and Next.js.

14
00:01:18,862 --> 00:01:20,964
Think of it as a lightweight Jira backend.

15
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:24,484
It has users, tasks, projects, and notification service.

16
00:01:25,070 --> 00:01:31,556
It is intentionally incomplete, about 30% built, and we are going to build it up together as we go through each section.

17
00:01:32,142 --> 00:01:34,948
By the end of the course, it will be a fully functional API.

18
00:01:35,374 --> 00:01:39,044
You can download the starting skeleton from the link in the description and follow along.

19
00:01:39,406 --> 00:01:40,560
Let's start with the foundation.

20
00:01:40,878 --> 00:01:43,748
Cursor is a code editor built on top of VS Code.

21
00:01:44,238 --> 00:01:46,692
Same shortcuts, same extensions, same interface.

22
00:01:47,534 --> 00:01:51,972
If you use VS Code today, switching takes about 5 minutes and you will feel right at home.

23
00:01:52,654 --> 00:01:57,380
When you first open it, you will see two views, an agentic view and an editor view.

24
00:01:58,062 --> 00:02:00,960
If you are coming from VS Code, switch to editor view.

25
00:02:01,262 --> 00:02:05,572
It is identical to what you are used to and you can start being productive immediately.

26
00:02:06,254 --> 00:02:10,308
Now here is the fundamental difference between cursor and every editor you have used before.

27
00:02:11,086 --> 00:02:13,252
A normal editor sees the file you have opened.

28
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:13,988
That is it.

29
00:02:14,734 --> 00:02:20,228
Cursor sees your entire code base, every function, every class, every relationship between files.

30
00:02:20,846 --> 00:02:25,060
And it uses that full context to help you in ways a traditional editor simply cannot.

31
00:02:25,710 --> 00:02:28,356
The mental model I want you to carry through the entire course is this.

32
00:02:29,134 --> 00:02:33,476
Think of the AI Inside Cursor as a brilliant engineer who just joined your team.

33
00:02:34,126 --> 00:02:42,948
They know every language, every framework, every design pattern, but they know nothing about your specific code base, your conventions, your legacy decisions, your folder structure.

34
00:02:43,566 --> 00:02:46,052
Without that knowledge, they default to generic patterns.

35
00:02:46,606 --> 00:02:50,532
Code that is syntactically correct but architecturally wrong for your project.

36
00:02:50,862 --> 00:02:52,708
It compiles, but it does not fit.

37
00:02:53,454 --> 00:02:58,404
Your job is to give them that context fast, and that is exactly what this course teaches you to do.

38
00:02:59,054 --> 00:03:02,436
Now before we go further, two quick things you should know about this setup.

39
00:03:02,830 --> 00:03:08,964
On model selection, if you are on the free plan, you get auto, which picks the right model for each task automatically.

40
00:03:09,550 --> 00:03:12,228
That is good enough to follow along with everything in this course.

41
00:03:12,814 --> 00:03:18,400
But if you are on the Pro Plan, at $20 a month you have access to models like Cloud, Sonnet, and Opus.

42
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:20,548
Use Sonnet for most tasks.

43
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:22,320
It is fast and accurate.

44
00:03:22,510 --> 00:03:28,580
Use Opus when you are doing something complex like designing architecture or debugging a subtle multi-file issue.

45
00:03:29,198 --> 00:03:31,172
For quick edits, auto is fine.

46
00:03:31,598 --> 00:03:37,920
On voice input, Cursor has built-in voice mode, and there is also a free tool called WhisperFlow that works well.

47
00:03:38,222 --> 00:03:41,796
So if you are writing long prompts, speaking them is significantly faster than typing.

48
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:43,364
Who was setting up early?

49
00:03:43,918 --> 00:03:49,860
Now there are three ways to interact with the AI inside Cursor, and each one is suited to a different type of task.

50
00:03:50,446 --> 00:03:52,388
Tab, Command K and Chat.

51
00:03:52,654 --> 00:03:55,236
Think of these as three levels of AI involvement.

52
00:03:55,760 --> 00:03:58,276
Tab is automatic and stays out of your way.

53
00:03:58,640 --> 00:04:00,004
Command K is targeted.

54
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:01,732
You pick exactly what to change.

55
00:04:02,190 --> 00:04:04,740
Chat is where you have a full conversation with your code base.

56
00:04:05,262 --> 00:04:09,796
As you go through the course, you'll naturally develop a feel for which one to reach for and when.

57
00:04:10,478 --> 00:04:15,940
Starting with tab, tab is cursor's autocomplete, but it works differently from what you are used to.

58
00:04:16,494 --> 00:04:19,972
Most editors predict the next few characters based on what you are typing.

59
00:04:20,494 --> 00:04:30,980
Cursor predicts your next edit based on what you are trying to do. It looks at the surrounding code, understands your intent, and suggests entire blocks, sometimes across multiple lines.

60
00:04:31,534 --> 00:04:33,412
You'll notice this the first time you use it.

61
00:04:33,870 --> 00:04:37,860
You finish one line, hit tab, and it writes the next three lines correctly.

62
00:04:38,350 --> 00:04:39,780
Hit tab again, done.

63
00:04:40,238 --> 00:04:42,788
It feels like the editor is reading your mind a little bit.

64
00:04:43,342 --> 00:04:46,756
And you will build that muscle memory fast because tab is always on.

65
00:04:47,168 --> 00:04:48,996
You do not have to activate anything.

66
00:04:49,582 --> 00:04:55,008
Point worth noting if the suggestion is wrong, just hit escape and keep trying and keep typing.

67
00:04:55,278 --> 00:04:56,200
Cursor will adjust.

68
00:04:56,590 --> 00:04:59,648
After you accept a tab suggestion, immediately press tab again.

69
00:04:59,886 --> 00:05:03,524
Cursor predicts the next logical change based on what you just accepted.

70
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:07,328
Change this together and you can move through repetitive code really fast.

71
00:05:07,694 --> 00:05:09,540
Most developers stop at one time.

72
00:05:09,902 --> 00:05:15,428
Don't. Now moving to Command K, and this is probably the one you'll use more than anything else day to day.

73
00:05:15,854 --> 00:05:18,084
I have our task management project open here.

74
00:05:18,606 --> 00:05:20,740
Let me show you exactly what Command K does.

75
00:05:21,230 --> 00:05:23,076
I'm going to open the notification service.

76
00:05:23,694 --> 00:05:26,852
You can see right away there is no error handling in any of these functions.

77
00:05:27,470 --> 00:05:30,020
This is one of the intentional gaps in our skeleton.

78
00:05:30,574 --> 00:05:36,208
I highlight this entire block, press Command K and type, add error handling to every function.

79
00:05:36,654 --> 00:05:41,412
Cursor shows me a dev, green for what is being added, red for what is being removed.

80
00:05:41,806 --> 00:05:43,204
I review it and accept.

81
00:05:43,502 --> 00:05:46,948
The whole thing took about 10 seconds and I never left the file.

82
00:05:47,406 --> 00:05:48,228
That is the pattern.

83
00:05:48,528 --> 00:05:50,340
Highlight, instruct, review.

84
00:05:50,638 --> 00:05:53,252
You can use it for anything targeted in a single file.

85
00:05:53,742 --> 00:05:55,172
Convert this to asyncavin.

86
00:05:55,534 --> 00:05:57,252
Add type annotations to this class.

87
00:05:57,870 --> 00:05:59,876
Refactor this to use the strategy pattern.

88
00:06:00,238 --> 00:06:03,620
Each time you get a precise diff on exactly what you are asked for.

89
00:06:03,982 --> 00:06:04,900
Nothing else changes.

90
00:06:05,390 --> 00:06:07,460
Now one thing most developers never discover.

91
00:06:07,950 --> 00:06:09,732
Command K works in the terminal too.

92
00:06:10,222 --> 00:06:14,468
Open the integrated terminal, press Command K and type in plain English.

93
00:06:14,830 --> 00:06:17,188
Show me the last five commit messages for this project.

94
00:06:17,648 --> 00:06:20,452
Or run all unit tests and lock the failures.

95
00:06:20,878 --> 00:06:22,404
It writes the shell command for you.

96
00:06:22,734 --> 00:06:25,412
Really useful when you cannot remember exact syntax.

97
00:06:25,934 --> 00:06:30,628
And one important boundary to know use command k for targeted changes in a single file.

98
00:06:30,894 --> 00:06:34,308
The moment your change involves more than one file, switch to chat.

99
00:06:34,702 --> 00:06:40,964
You will get worse results if you push Command K beyond what it is designed for. So that covers tab and command-k.

100
00:06:41,518 --> 00:06:44,900
Now let's talk about chat, which is where things get more powerful.

101
00:06:45,646 --> 00:06:47,492
Command L opens the chat panel.

102
00:06:47,982 --> 00:06:54,180
You'll use this whenever you need reasoning, multi-file changes, or want to ask questions about how your code works.

103
00:06:54,766 --> 00:06:58,372
The key difference between this and something like ChatGPT is context.

104
00:06:58,734 --> 00:07:02,404
When you ask cursor something here, it is not answering from general knowledge alone.

105
00:07:02,926 --> 00:07:08,228
It has full awareness of your code base, the files, the structure, the relationships between things.

106
00:07:08,782 --> 00:07:10,500
But here is something important to understand.

107
00:07:10,894 --> 00:07:15,364
Cursor does not automatically know which part of your code base is relevant to your question.

108
00:07:15,790 --> 00:07:16,644
You have to tell it.

109
00:07:16,878 --> 00:07:19,044
And that is what the add system is for.

110
00:07:19,374 --> 00:07:25,764
And getting good at this is honestly what separate developers who get great results from developers who get inconsistent results.

111
00:07:26,382 --> 00:07:34,084
So type at the rate inside the chat and you can reference specific files, folders, official documentation, your git history, your terminal output,

112
00:07:34,478 --> 00:07:35,748
even live web content.

113
00:07:36,014 --> 00:07:37,924
Let me show you what this looks like in our project.

114
00:07:38,414 --> 00:07:40,132
There is an error in the terminal right now.

115
00:07:40,494 --> 00:07:43,204
The task management notification is failing silently.

116
00:07:43,790 --> 00:07:47,716
So I open a new chat, type add terminal to pull in the error output.

117
00:07:48,270 --> 00:07:52,132
Then add notification service.js to bring in the relevant file.

118
00:07:52,718 --> 00:07:53,764
And I describe the problem.

119
00:07:54,350 --> 00:07:56,644
Cursor has both the error and the code.

120
00:07:57,038 --> 00:07:58,820
It identifies the issue immediately.

121
00:07:59,246 --> 00:08:04,804
No copy pasting the error into separate window, no context switching, everything is right here.

122
00:08:05,454 --> 00:08:09,700
Now, there is one principle I want you to remember, and it applies throughout the entire course.

123
00:08:10,094 --> 00:08:12,580
Bigger context does not mean better answers.

124
00:08:13,230 --> 00:08:14,564
Relevance beats volume.

125
00:08:14,894 --> 00:08:21,796
If you dump your entire code base into context for simple bug fix, you'll get a worse answer than if you reference just the two files involved.

126
00:08:22,158 --> 00:08:25,540
The model gets confused by noise the same way a human engineer would.

127
00:08:25,870 --> 00:08:36,036
So if you want someone to fix a bug in the notification service, you do not hand them the entire codebase and say, hey, go figure it out. You say, here is the file, here is the error, fix it.

128
00:08:36,494 --> 00:08:41,124
Always open a new chat for every new task. This is not just about saving tokens.

129
00:08:41,678 --> 00:08:42,980
This is the shallow reason.

130
00:08:43,182 --> 00:08:49,284
The real reason is that stale context from a previous task causes the agent to make assumptions based on the wrong problem.

131
00:08:49,678 --> 00:08:52,580
It is the number one cause of a bad output in cursor.

132
00:08:53,006 --> 00:08:54,404
One task, one chat.

133
00:08:54,672 --> 00:09:00,932
Make it a habit from day one. When you are debugging, combine add terminal and add screenshot in the same message.

134
00:09:01,358 --> 00:09:05,028
Give it the error output and a screenshot of what you are seeing in the browser.

135
00:09:05,392 --> 00:09:12,836
That combination cuts the back and forth significantly because the model has both the technical signal and the visual context at the same time.

136
00:09:13,262 --> 00:09:16,644
And here is a workflow we are going to use ourselves later in this course.

137
00:09:17,102 --> 00:09:25,072
If you need to repeat a pattern across multiple services, say you add error handling to the notification service in the last commit and now want the same in

138
00:09:25,232 --> 00:09:34,752
all the services, open a new chat and say, Look at what we did in a commit ABC123 and implement the same error handling pattern in email service.js.

139
00:09:35,310 --> 00:09:38,020
Cursor reads the dev and mirrors the pattern exactly.

140
00:09:38,446 --> 00:09:41,156
No copy paste, no re-explaining from scratch.

141
00:09:41,710 --> 00:09:46,308
So to summarize, tab when you are in flow and want the AI to keep up with you.

142
00:09:46,894 --> 00:09:50,308
Command K when you know exactly what needs to change in one place.

143
00:09:51,022 --> 00:09:54,500
Chat when you need reasoning or changes across multiple files.

144
00:09:55,182 --> 00:10:00,196
Get comfortable with these three and you'll already be more productive than most developers using cursor.

145
00:10:01,102 --> 00:10:05,412
Alright, we have fixed the notification service error handling and debugged a terminal issue.

146
00:10:05,838 --> 00:10:07,044
The project is coming along.

147
00:10:07,310 --> 00:10:10,512
Now let's talk about how to build entire features end-to-end.

148
00:10:10,990 --> 00:10:12,612
Plan mode and agent mode.

149
00:10:13,134 --> 00:10:19,684
Now, most developers open cursor, switch straight to agent mode, describe what they want and let it run.

150
00:10:20,078 --> 00:10:22,628
And most of the time they end up in a correction loop.

151
00:10:22,992 --> 00:10:25,700
The agent did something reasonable but not quite right.

152
00:10:26,158 --> 00:10:31,460
They correct it, it drifts again, they correct again, it gets frustrating fast.

153
00:10:32,014 --> 00:10:35,972
The fix is plan mode, and once you use it properly, you will not go back.

154
00:10:36,366 --> 00:10:37,092
Here is what I mean.

155
00:10:37,710 --> 00:10:40,356
We need to add a task labels feature to our project.

156
00:10:40,878 --> 00:10:45,444
The ability to tag tasks with labels like urgent, blocked, in review.

157
00:10:45,998 --> 00:10:49,956
This involves front end changes, back end changes, and a database migration.

158
00:10:50,350 --> 00:10:58,180
Exactly the kind of task where agent mode alone, without clear direction, will produce something that works but does not fit the rest of the code base.

159
00:10:58,958 --> 00:11:02,180
So I switch the chat to plan mode and describe what I want.

160
00:11:02,798 --> 00:11:04,836
Cursor does not write a single line of code yet.

161
00:11:05,358 --> 00:11:12,868
Instead, it generates an architecture overview, the database schema changes, and a complete checklist of every step it plans to take.

162
00:11:13,550 --> 00:11:20,356
I can read through this, edit it, add a to-do item, remove something that is out of scope and adjust the approach.

163
00:11:20,878 --> 00:11:24,740
The plan is something I own and I can change before anything gets touched.

164
00:11:25,486 --> 00:11:27,716
Once the plan looks right, I hit build.

165
00:11:28,142 --> 00:11:32,516
That is the moment cursor switches automatically into agent mode and starts executing.

166
00:11:32,974 --> 00:11:42,788
It works through the checklist, writes the database migration, updates the task model, adds the API endpoints, updates the front end, and checks off each item as it goes.

167
00:11:43,182 --> 00:11:46,308
I'm not doing anything except watching and occasionally reviewing.

168
00:11:46,830 --> 00:11:49,284
Now here is something important to understand about how this works.

169
00:11:49,838 --> 00:11:52,740
The code is being written into your actual files as it goes.

170
00:11:53,134 --> 00:11:56,004
It already exists on disk the moment the agent writes it.

171
00:11:56,846 --> 00:11:58,980
So before you hit keep all, test first.

172
00:11:59,502 --> 00:12:03,012
Run the app, check the behavior, make sure it is doing what you expected.

173
00:12:03,406 --> 00:12:04,132
Then accept.

174
00:12:04,494 --> 00:12:06,948
If something went wrong, you have the undo all button.

175
00:12:07,246 --> 00:12:09,572
But that window closes as you keep building.

176
00:12:10,062 --> 00:12:13,028
Which is why you test before accepting, not after.

177
00:12:13,550 --> 00:12:16,708
For anything non trivial, always start with plan mode.

178
00:12:17,070 --> 00:12:21,028
It forces you to think clearly about what you want before the agent touches your code.

179
00:12:21,614 --> 00:12:24,580
Vague instructions in agent mode lead to vague output.

180
00:12:25,038 --> 00:12:28,100
A clear plan upfront saves multiple correction cycles.

181
00:12:29,070 --> 00:12:34,756
If you already have requirements document or a PRD, bring it with add file at the start of plan mode.

182
00:12:35,054 --> 00:12:38,180
Cursor reads it and builds the plan directly from your requirements.

183
00:12:38,798 --> 00:12:40,388
You skip all the clarifying questions.

184
00:12:41,294 --> 00:12:44,484
And this is the single most important habit around agent mode.

185
00:12:44,686 --> 00:12:48,228
Commit your code before you run the agent, not after, before.

186
00:12:48,526 --> 00:12:51,780
Right now, before I hit build on this plan, I am making a git commit.

187
00:12:52,048 --> 00:12:57,348
If the agent touches 15 files and goes in a direction I do not want, I have a clean revert point.

188
00:12:57,710 --> 00:12:59,332
And this will save you at some point.

189
00:12:59,598 --> 00:13:00,516
I can guarantee that.

190
00:13:00,942 --> 00:13:04,612
Once the agent finishes, there is a built-in quality gate worth using.

191
00:13:05,006 --> 00:13:10,148
One model wrote the code, you can ask a second model to review those changes before you accept them.

192
00:13:10,510 --> 00:13:12,292
It catches things the first model missed.

193
00:13:12,688 --> 00:13:15,300
No extra tools, it is right there in the interface.

194
00:13:16,046 --> 00:13:17,956
The task label feature is done and tested.

195
00:13:18,382 --> 00:13:20,708
The project is now about 55% complete.

196
00:13:21,102 --> 00:13:28,708
We have auth, task card, error handling in the notification service, and now full task labels with front end and backend wired up.

197
00:13:29,088 --> 00:13:32,516
Everything we have built so far, we did it through individual interactions.

198
00:13:32,974 --> 00:13:35,396
One chat, one task, one developer.

199
00:13:35,950 --> 00:13:42,148
But if you are on a team or even just working on the same project over several months, you run into a different problem.

200
00:13:42,702 --> 00:13:45,124
The agent does not remember anything between sessions.

201
00:13:45,550 --> 00:13:47,204
Every new chart is a blank slate.

202
00:13:47,566 --> 00:13:50,596
It does not know your team uses async database calls everywhere.

203
00:13:50,990 --> 00:13:52,836
It does not know your folder conventions.

204
00:13:53,166 --> 00:13:57,412
It does not know there is a legacy auth module that should never be modified directly.

205
00:13:58,094 --> 00:14:02,788
Rules, commands, and memories are how you fix that. Let's go through each one.

206
00:14:03,438 --> 00:14:04,676
Starting with rules.

207
00:14:04,910 --> 00:14:10,180
A rule is a persistent instruction that the agent reads automatically before every interaction in your project.

208
00:14:10,542 --> 00:14:15,236
You write it once and it applies everywhere without you repeating yourself in every chat.

209
00:14:15,598 --> 00:14:19,088
Think of it as the onboarding talk you would give a new engineer on day one.

210
00:14:19,374 --> 00:14:23,492
Your coding standards, your architectural decisions, your do's and don'ts.

211
00:14:23,886 --> 00:14:26,884
The fastest way to get started is to type generate in the chat.

212
00:14:27,182 --> 00:14:34,020
Cursor scans your existing codebase, the patterns you are already using, the libraries you have imported, the way your files are structured,

213
00:14:34,318 --> 00:14:37,380
and generates a rules file that matches your project automatically.

214
00:14:37,614 --> 00:14:39,300
Let me do this on our project right now.

215
00:14:39,502 --> 00:14:40,900
I type slash /generate.

216
00:14:41,198 --> 00:14:48,388
Cursor analyzes the code base and in about 30 seconds it has produced a solid set of rules that reflect what we have already built.

217
00:14:48,688 --> 00:14:56,516
I review them, make a few adjustments, and now every future agent interaction on this project follows these rules without me having to mention them.

218
00:14:56,782 --> 00:15:02,848
Instead of writing rules from scratch, let cursor read your existing code and generate them. Then customize from there.

219
00:15:03,086 --> 00:15:07,908
Now once you have a base, there is a community resource called cursor.directory worth bookmarking.

220
00:15:08,334 --> 00:15:16,068
It has battle-tested rules for specific stacks Next.js, TypeScript, Python, React, UI, and UX patterns and more.

221
00:15:16,494 --> 00:15:21,412
Instead of figuring out best practices from scratch, you find the rule set that matches your stack.

222
00:15:21,710 --> 00:15:23,300
Bring it in and adjust it.

223
00:15:23,598 --> 00:15:26,340
Someone has already done the hard work for your exact setup.

224
00:15:26,990 --> 00:15:31,236
In the cursor directory, we can see some well-documented templates written by developers.

225
00:15:31,534 --> 00:15:33,188
Like for the next JS project we have.

226
00:15:33,742 --> 00:15:37,636
You are an expert in web development, include JS, TS and CSS.

227
00:15:38,158 --> 00:15:43,460
Just have a look into it and try it out. You can also scope rules to specific parts of your project.

228
00:15:43,950 --> 00:15:50,608
If I only want a rule to apply to Node.js backend files, I set the glob pattern to backend slash start.js.

229
00:15:51,086 --> 00:15:54,564
Frontend rules stay on the front end, backend rules stay on the back end.

230
00:15:54,830 --> 00:15:58,852
Irrelevant rules add noise and noise reduces our output quality.

231
00:15:59,214 --> 00:16:01,248
There are three types of rules worth knowing about.

232
00:16:01,488 --> 00:16:06,884
Project rules live in your git repo and apply to every developer on the team when they pull.

233
00:16:07,328 --> 00:16:12,036
User rules are personal preferences that stay on your machine and never get committed.

234
00:16:12,398 --> 00:16:15,268
For team conventions, always use project rules.

235
00:16:15,694 --> 00:16:20,868
Now let's talk about commands because this is where individual productivity becomes team productivity.

236
00:16:21,326 --> 00:16:26,468
A command is a reusable instruction you save as a markdown file and trigger it with a slash.

237
00:16:26,798 --> 00:16:30,244
The best way to understand this is through a concrete example from our project.

238
00:16:30,702 --> 00:16:37,060
I need to add structured logging to multiple services the task service, the notification service, and the email service.

239
00:16:37,582 --> 00:16:42,916
I could write a detailed prompt every single time explaining exactly how I want the logging structured.

240
00:16:43,248 --> 00:16:51,172
Or I can write that prompt once, save it as the command, and from then on type slash add logging and point it at any file.

241
00:16:51,534 --> 00:16:52,516
Let me create this now.

242
00:16:52,782 --> 00:17:00,260
I go to the commands folder inside.cursor, create a new file called addlogging.md and write out the exact instructions.

243
00:17:00,686 --> 00:17:11,088
Import the login library, create a logger object, add a log line at the start of every function with relevant context, add error logging in every exception block, and I include a

244
00:17:11,200 --> 00:17:12,580
simple snippet as a reference.

245
00:17:12,974 --> 00:17:14,148
Command is ready.

246
00:17:14,542 --> 00:17:20,208
Now I open a new chat, type slash add logging, reference the notification service, and hit enter.

247
00:17:20,526 --> 00:17:23,940
Cursor reads the command file and implements the pattern consistently.

248
00:17:24,430 --> 00:17:25,604
Same structure every time.

249
00:17:26,126 --> 00:17:29,028
Now I do the same for the task service and email service.

250
00:17:29,486 --> 00:17:32,868
Three services, same logging pattern, no repeated prompts.

251
00:17:33,326 --> 00:17:41,252
If you find yourself writing the same type of prompt more than twice, adding logging, writing test, migrating an API version, save it as a command.

252
00:17:41,550 --> 00:17:45,412
You write the detailed instructions once and reuse them indefinitely.

253
00:17:45,806 --> 00:17:49,092
The commands folder lives inside.cursor in your repository.

254
00:17:49,518 --> 00:17:54,340
Commit it to git and every developer who pulls the repo gets all your commands automatically.

255
00:17:54,830 --> 00:18:01,220
A new developer joins, pulls a repo, and immediately has access to every reusable workflow your team has built.

256
00:18:01,550 --> 00:18:06,564
So to bring this section together, rules encode your standards so the agent follows them automatically.

257
00:18:06,926 --> 00:18:10,052
Commands make your best workflows reusable across the whole team.

258
00:18:10,670 --> 00:18:15,268
Memories give the agent persistent context so you are not starting from scratch every session.

259
00:18:15,726 --> 00:18:17,824
Set these up at the start of every project.

260
00:18:18,094 --> 00:18:24,164
It takes about 30 minutes the first time and pays back across every single agent interaction from that point forward.

261
00:18:24,430 --> 00:18:26,224
Our project is now at about 70%.

262
00:18:26,734 --> 00:18:34,884
Error handling in place, task levels build, structured logging consistent across all services, and the agent now has the right rules to follow going forward.

263
00:18:35,118 --> 00:18:37,156
Now let's get into the advanced workflows.

264
00:18:37,518 --> 00:18:40,676
The stuff that will genuinely change how much you can shape in a day.

265
00:18:41,038 --> 00:18:44,612
Everything up to this point has been one agent, one task, one developer.

266
00:18:44,864 --> 00:18:47,140
That alone is a significantly productivity gain.

267
00:18:47,424 --> 00:18:50,372
But there is another level, and that is what the section covers.

268
00:18:50,862 --> 00:18:55,172
Let's start with the parallel agents because this changes how you think about your workday.

269
00:18:55,630 --> 00:19:02,564
Cursor lets you run multiple agents at the same time, each one working on completely separate tasks in its own isolated workspace.

270
00:19:03,054 --> 00:19:04,964
Under the hood, it uses Git work trees.

271
00:19:05,344 --> 00:19:09,700
Each agent gets its own branch and cannot interfere with what the others are doing.

272
00:19:10,030 --> 00:19:12,324
Here is what this looks like on our project right now.

273
00:19:12,544 --> 00:19:13,744
I have three things to do.

274
00:19:14,030 --> 00:19:23,332
Add pagination to the task list API, write unit test for the notification service, and fix the task counter bug that has been in the skeleton since the start.

275
00:19:23,584 --> 00:19:25,636
Normally that is a sequential afternoon.

276
00:19:25,838 --> 00:19:28,068
I kick off all three agents simultaneously.

277
00:19:28,462 --> 00:19:34,788
While agent 1 is implementing pagination, agent 2 is writing test, agent 3 is tracking down the bug.

278
00:19:35,214 --> 00:19:37,412
I review all three outputs when they are done.

279
00:19:37,838 --> 00:19:40,580
That sequential afternoon just became about an hour.

280
00:19:41,166 --> 00:19:44,644
Use parallel agents for tasks that are genuinely independent of each other.

281
00:19:45,038 --> 00:19:48,356
A new feature, a bug fix, and a test suite are perfect candidates.

282
00:19:48,814 --> 00:19:52,324
They touch different parts of the code base and do not need to coordinate.

283
00:19:52,622 --> 00:19:55,748
Do not run parallel agents on tasks that depend on each other's output.

284
00:19:56,110 --> 00:19:59,140
That creates merge conflicts that cost more time than you save.

285
00:19:59,406 --> 00:20:02,820
Now let's talk about background agents, which takes it even further.

286
00:20:03,086 --> 00:20:05,988
Background agents run in the cloud, not on your machine.

287
00:20:06,350 --> 00:20:15,344
You describe a task, cursor spins up a remote, isolated environment, clones your repository, works on a separate branch, and when it is done, it produces a merge ready

288
00:20:15,504 --> 00:20:19,300
pull request with screenshots, logs, and a recording of what it did.

289
00:20:19,630 --> 00:20:22,212
You can trigger a background agent and close your laptop.

290
00:20:22,384 --> 00:20:23,268
It keeps working.

291
00:20:23,534 --> 00:20:26,148
You come back and there is a PR waiting for your review.

292
00:20:26,446 --> 00:20:30,692
In our project, I can say implement error handling in all the APIs of the project.

293
00:20:31,086 --> 00:20:36,260
The background agent picks that up, works through it, and produces a PR I review like any other.

294
00:20:36,654 --> 00:20:39,044
The keyword with background agent is well defined.

295
00:20:39,374 --> 00:20:43,972
A vague instruction produces a vague PR that takes longer to review than it's saved.

296
00:20:44,270 --> 00:20:46,532
So treat it like writing a proper ticket.

297
00:20:46,798 --> 00:20:49,252
Clear scope, clear acceptance criteria.

298
00:20:49,518 --> 00:20:51,716
And you do not have to open cursor to trigger one.

299
00:20:51,984 --> 00:20:54,692
You can do it directly from Slack, GitHub, and Linear.

300
00:20:54,894 --> 00:21:01,188
If your team manages to work in Linear, assign a ticket to a background agent right from there and it starts working immediately.

301
00:21:01,646 --> 00:21:05,572
So background agents are powerful, but I want you to notice what actually happened there.

302
00:21:06,126 --> 00:21:07,300
Your code left your machine.

303
00:21:07,630 --> 00:21:10,500
Cursor cloned your repository into its own cloud to do the work.

304
00:21:11,150 --> 00:21:16,260
For most projects, you might not mind that your data runs in cursor servers under their control and visibility.

305
00:21:16,814 --> 00:21:20,324
But there is a whole category of code where that single step is not allowed.

306
00:21:21,070 --> 00:21:29,028
If you work on a banking system, a healthcare app, anything where the code itself is sensitive, your security team will not let you send it to an outside cloud.

307
00:21:29,678 --> 00:21:32,676
I spent years on code bases like that at PayPal and Capital One.

308
00:21:33,134 --> 00:21:37,220
On those repos, a tool that clones your code to the cloud gets blocked on day one.

309
00:21:37,870 --> 00:21:40,132
That is exact problem today's sponsor is built for.

310
00:21:41,166 --> 00:21:46,116
Origin enables agentic software development secured from third-party data retention and exploitation.

311
00:21:46,478 --> 00:21:47,396
The idea is simple.

312
00:21:47,790 --> 00:21:51,428
In the origin IDE, you can build locally or on the confidential cloud.

313
00:21:52,014 --> 00:21:56,228
In confidential cloud mode, you get the same kind of cloud agent workflow you just saw.

314
00:21:56,750 --> 00:22:05,156
But your code runs inside a sealed hardware isolated environment that you can actually verify is encrypted from them, the model providers, and the cloud providers.

315
00:22:05,934 --> 00:22:08,260
It starts in Origin Studio, the web console.

316
00:22:08,686 --> 00:22:13,252
I connect the repo and Origin reads the code base and turns it into a clean list of tasks.

317
00:22:14,030 --> 00:22:17,412
I pick a task and launch a confidential session right from that list.

318
00:22:17,742 --> 00:22:21,060
That session opens in Origin CD, the desktop ID.

319
00:22:21,614 --> 00:22:25,316
Remember how Cursor uses work trees to isolate parallel agents on your machine?

320
00:22:25,838 --> 00:22:31,172
Origin uses the same work tree idea, but each one runs inside a hardware sealed sandbox in the cloud.

321
00:22:31,598 --> 00:22:33,188
Now I work exactly like before.

322
00:22:33,614 --> 00:22:36,452
I give it the same task we gave the background agent earlier.

323
00:22:37,070 --> 00:22:38,980
Add error handling to all the APIs.

324
00:22:39,758 --> 00:22:44,964
The agent edits the files and runs the commands, but all of that is happening inside the sealed environment.

325
00:22:45,294 --> 00:22:48,356
Your code, your prompts, and your secrets are never exposed.

326
00:22:48,974 --> 00:22:50,884
And the model do not keep anything you send.

327
00:22:51,310 --> 00:22:55,076
Nothing is stored and nothing is used for training by origin or by the model provider.

328
00:22:55,534 --> 00:22:58,500
And here's the part that makes this different from a normal cloud agent.

329
00:22:58,864 --> 00:23:00,164
You can ask for proof.

330
00:23:00,686 --> 00:23:04,644
One click gives you a report that shows your code actually ran inside that sealed environment.

331
00:23:04,974 --> 00:23:08,836
And once the work is done, the environment is torn down, so nothing is left behind.

332
00:23:09,422 --> 00:23:12,292
And if you do not even want the cloud, there is a local mode.

333
00:23:12,782 --> 00:23:18,180
You download Origin CDE on your Mac, open your local files, and the agent works fully on your laptop.

334
00:23:18,510 --> 00:23:20,260
No cloud and no GitHub needed.

335
00:23:20,590 --> 00:23:25,604
So if your team has ever blocked a cloud AI tool for security reasons, this is the one to look at.

336
00:23:25,870 --> 00:23:28,064
The link is in the description and you can sign up there.

337
00:23:28,238 --> 00:23:31,268
Moving on to Bugbot, which is one of my favorite features in Cursor.

338
00:23:31,598 --> 00:23:36,612
Bugbot connects to your GitHub repository and automatically reviews every pull request you open.

339
00:23:36,784 --> 00:23:43,236
It scans the changes, identifies potential bugs, and leaves comments directly on the PR, exactly like a human reviewer would.

340
00:23:43,504 --> 00:23:46,340
When it finds an issue, it adds a fix-in cursor link.

341
00:23:46,766 --> 00:23:50,704
You click it, cursor opens with a pre-filled prompt pointing directly to the problem.

342
00:23:50,958 --> 00:23:52,036
You fix it and push.

343
00:23:52,750 --> 00:23:54,308
Think about what this means at scale.

344
00:23:54,624 --> 00:23:56,868
Every PR gets an immediate review pass.

345
00:23:57,230 --> 00:24:03,652
Your human reviewers spend their time on architecture and logic rather than catching missing null checks and unhandled exceptions.

346
00:24:04,302 --> 00:24:06,624
Set a bug bot on every active repository.

347
00:24:06,862 --> 00:24:10,436
5 minutes to configure runs automatically from that point forward.

348
00:24:10,798 --> 00:24:13,508
Now let's talk about MCP, Model Context Protocol.

349
00:24:13,678 --> 00:24:17,796
And this is what turns cursor from a great editor into a connected development environment.

350
00:24:18,064 --> 00:24:20,292
MCP is a plugin system for Cursor's agent.

351
00:24:20,464 --> 00:24:26,276
It lets the agent connect to external tools and services like GitHub, Notion, Figma, Stripe, Playwright, Databases.

352
00:24:26,624 --> 00:24:29,220
There are over 1800 MCP servers available.

353
00:24:29,486 --> 00:24:35,332
When you add one, the agent can interact with that tool directly from within your workflow without you switching context.

354
00:24:35,598 --> 00:24:38,464
The most immediately useful one to install is Context7.

355
00:24:38,766 --> 00:24:39,264
Here is why.

356
00:24:39,534 --> 00:24:44,932
When Cursor writes code using a framework like Fast API or Next.js, it draws on its training data.

357
00:24:45,230 --> 00:24:47,268
But that training data has a cutoff date.

358
00:24:47,502 --> 00:24:53,316
If a framework released a significant update 6 months ago, cursor might still be generating code against the old API.

359
00:24:53,614 --> 00:24:57,380
That code compiles, it runs, but it uses deprecated patterns.

360
00:24:57,646 --> 00:24:59,620
You find out when something breaks in production.

361
00:24:59,918 --> 00:25:06,180
Context 7 is an MCV server that always has the latest documentation for major frameworks, updated daily.

362
00:25:06,510 --> 00:25:15,620
When you add it and set a rule that says always use context 7 for API documentation, the agent fetches current docs before writing any framework-specific code.

363
00:25:16,046 --> 00:25:18,660
You stop getting outdated patterns automatically.

364
00:25:19,022 --> 00:25:25,700
So install context 7 as your first MCP server, one-click setup, then add that rule to your rules file and you are done.

365
00:25:26,126 --> 00:25:29,924
After that, add MCP servers incrementally, not all at once.

366
00:25:30,350 --> 00:25:32,144
Start with the tools you use every day.

367
00:25:32,558 --> 00:25:38,084
GitHub for PR management, Notion if that is what your specs live, playwrite if you write end-to-end test.

368
00:25:38,384 --> 00:25:40,708
Each one removes a context switch from your workflow.

369
00:25:41,070 --> 00:25:43,140
The last thing I want to talk about is hooks.

370
00:25:43,470 --> 00:25:47,140
A hook lets you run your own scripts at specific points in the agent loop.

371
00:25:47,424 --> 00:25:51,044
After every file edit, before a command runs, and after a commit.

372
00:25:51,278 --> 00:25:53,024
Our project is now at about 80%.

373
00:25:53,486 --> 00:25:56,868
Pagination is in, tests are written, the counter bug is fixed.

374
00:25:57,198 --> 00:26:06,852
Bugbot is catching issues on every PR, and the agent is pulling current documentation through context 7. That is a realistic end state for a real project using a real workflow.

375
00:26:07,104 --> 00:26:10,340
Your job as a developer is no longer to write every line of code.

376
00:26:10,830 --> 00:26:16,100
Your job is to give the right context, set the right constraints, and validate the output.

377
00:26:16,660 --> 00:26:20,280
Intent, context, constraints, validate, repeat.

378
00:26:20,910 --> 00:26:22,244
The agent executes.

379
00:26:22,592 --> 00:26:23,940
You guide and review.

380
00:26:24,238 --> 00:26:28,676
The developers who get 10x more productive with cursor are not the ones who know the most shortcuts.

381
00:26:29,152 --> 00:26:36,516
They are the ones who give better context, write better rules, build better commands, and use plan mode before they let the agent run.

382
00:26:36,846 --> 00:26:40,356
That skill gets more valuable as the model gets better, not less.

383
00:26:40,686 --> 00:26:43,012
One last thing, do not go on full autopilot.

384
00:26:43,182 --> 00:26:46,820
Review what the agent writes, understand the changes before you accept them.

385
00:26:47,022 --> 00:26:51,632
The code that shifts to production is your responsibility regardless of who or what wrote it.

386
00:26:51,758 --> 00:26:54,660
I have linked the project skeleton we use throughout the course in the description.

387
00:26:55,022 --> 00:26:59,780
Download it, open it in cursor, and try these tips as you work through the project yourself.

388
00:26:59,952 --> 00:27:02,832
That is really the best way to get comfortable with all of this.

389
00:27:03,086 --> 00:27:04,672
I'll see you in the next one.

